Tinea Corporis / Ringworm (Child)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Annular scaly plaques, Central clearing, Advancing active border

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-042
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful secondary excoriation and bleeding from a rapidly spreading annular rash, causing severe distress in a pediatric patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient with an intensely itchy, spreading circular rash that had been scratched raw, resulting in focal bleeding and crying. The parent was highly anxious about a rapidly spreading "flesh-eating" infection. Urgent assessment was required to definitively diagnose the rash, rule out an aggressive bacterial cellulitis, and provide immediate topical treatment and symptom control to stop the self-harming itch cycle before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "These red circles are spreading all over his legs, and he is scratching them so hard they are bleeding. He won't stop crying from the itch."
History of Present Illness: The 7-year-old patient developed a small red, scaly patch on the right thigh 10 days ago. Over the last 5 days, it has rapidly expanded into large, annular erythematous plaques with a prominent scaly advancing border. There is distinct central clearing within the annular lesions. The parent reports an outward centrifugal expansion of the lesions over time, now affecting both legs. The rash is causing intense localized pruritus. The child plays heavily in soil and grass. The severe itching has led to violent scratching, causing superficial bleeding and weeping over the active margins. The parent fears an aggressive bacterial infection.
Functional Status: Independent pediatric patient, highly distressed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation and self-inflicted skin trauma due to severe pruritus.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 26 Kg
General: Agitated, tearful child, actively trying to scratch the legs despite parental restraint.
Physical Exam: The bilateral anterior thighs and calves demonstrate multiple 4-8 cm annular scaly plaques. The lesions feature a raised, highly erythematous, advancing active border with distinct central clearing. The skin inside the rings is slightly hyperpigmented. Due to intense scratching, the advancing borders of several plaques exhibit raw, weeping excoriations with pinpoint bleeding and minor serosanguinous crusting. However, there is no deep, hot, indurated swelling, no thick honey-coloured crusting (impetigo), and the child is afebrile.
Environmental Observations: Patient has visible dirt under fingernails from outdoor play.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tinea Corporis with acute secondary excoriation - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Bacterial Cellulitis / Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The highly classic annular shape with central clearing and prominent scaling on the leading edge. The bleeding is purely mechanical from scratching, lacking the hot, uniform, spreading induration of cellulitis.

Granuloma Annulare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense pruritus and presence of a scaly surface; granuloma annulare is typically asymptomatic, smooth, and lacks epidermal scaling.

Nummular Eczema - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct central clearing and advancing raised border, contrasting with the uniform inflammation of coin-shaped eczema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The pediatric patient presents with severe Tinea Corporis complicated by acute, painful excoriations driven by intense pruritus. Triage accurately flagged the bleeding and severe parental anxiety regarding a spreading infection as an acute out-of-hours scenario. Immediate on-site assessment was critical to differentiate this highly pruritic superficial fungal infection from a more dangerous bacterial cellulitis. By confirming the diagnosis of ringworm, providing immediate topical relief for the raw skin, and prescribing targeted fungicidal therapy, the intervention immediately de-escalated the parents' severe panic and prevented further self-inflicted tissue damage by the child.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansed the excoriated areas with sterile saline. Prescribed topical Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied BD to the lesions. Prescribed a mild topical corticosteroid (Hydrocortisone 1% cream) to be applied over the Terbinafine strictly for 3 days to rapidly reduce the intense itch and inflammation. Advised oral Cetirizine 5mg stat for systemic itch control.
Positive Behavioral Change: By gently applying the anti-fungal cream specifically to the raised red edges twice a day, you will instantly poison the fungus causing the rash. By keeping your child's fingernails clipped extremely short and giving the anti-itch medicine tonight, you will physically stop them from tearing their skin open, allowing the bleeding scratches to heal painlessly while they sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days to ensure the fungal infection is clearing and the excoriations have healed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the red rings become deeply swollen, hot, and start leaking thick yellow pus, or if the child develops a fever over 38.0C, you must present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The fungus is easily spread by direct contact. The redness may temporarily look slightly worse when the steroid is stopped, but the anti-fungal will cure it.
Clinical Course: The extreme itch and weeping will stop within 48 hours. The red rings will slowly flatten and fade over 2-3 weeks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure the anti-fungal cream is continued for a full week after the skin looks completely normal.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your child caught a common soil fungus. It grows outward in a ring, causing severe irritation. The anti-fungal cream kills the root cause, and the short 3-day course of mild steroid will act like a fire extinguisher to instantly stop the agonizing itch and let the scratched skin heal.
Actionable Steps: Wash the child's hands and scrub under their fingernails thoroughly. Do not share towels or bedding.

